# Authority vs presentation

Visual/UI work must **not** casually change gameplay authority.

Keep separate:
1. Server/database authority (Postgres RPCs, RLS)
2. Client state
3. Presentation/rendering

Do not move authoritative calculations into browser JS just because a UI change is easier.

Must stay server-authoritative (examples):
capture settlement · inventory grants/removal · PokéCoins · Pass claims · Mart buy/sell · release · Oak transfer · Evolution Candy · evolution · PC destructive ops · cosmetic entitlement · rankings eligibility · Twitch identity mapping

UI may preview safe authoritative results; final mutation is always server-validated.

## Realtime isolation

Do not mix unrelated presentation refactors into realtime/state authority code.
Keep Supabase/Twitch/realtime in established controllers; pass normalized data into presentation.
Improve separation incrementally when touching that code — no big-bang rewrite for style alone.

## Destructive ops

- Explicit confirmation matching severity
- Never client-only confirmation for permanent loss
- Server must validate destructive intent
- Race conditions must not silently increase destruction
- Never silently release/delete Pokémon to “fix” an error
